{"cell_type":"In","gene_symbol":"ANTXR1","gene_symbol_current":"ANTXR1","gene_id":"ENSG00000169604","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.837018,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0987168,"nucleus_score":-0.579817,"pct_ad":0.157941,"pct_control":0.162154,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999889,"donor_log2fc":-0.0531033,"donor_lfcse":0.156201,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":6.05294e-07},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"ANTXR1","gene_symbol_current":"ANTXR1","gene_id":"ENSG00000169604","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.519912,"nucleus_log2fc":0.171882,"nucleus_score":1.23172,"pct_ad":0.298273,"pct_control":0.281226,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.940764,"donor_log2fc":0.222449,"donor_lfcse":0.116899,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00361274}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000169604","module":"black","kme":0.201862,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.56571,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000169604","module":"green","kme":0.111494,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.416898,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000169604","module":"brown","kme":0.130529,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.372712,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000169604","entrez_id":["84168"],"uniprot":["Q9H6X2"],"ncbi_summary":"This gene encodes a type I transmembrane protein and is a tumor-specific endothelial marker that has been implicated in colorectal cancer. The encoded protein has been shown to also be a docking protein or receptor for Bacillus anthracis toxin, the causative agent of the disease, anthrax. The binding of the protective antigen (PA) component, of the tripartite anthrax toxin, to this receptor protein mediates delivery of toxin components to the cytosol of cells. Once inside the cell, the other two components of anthrax toxin, edema factor (EF) and lethal factor (LF) disrupt normal cellular processes. Three alternatively spliced variants that encode different protein isoforms have been described. [provided by RefSeq, Oct 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q9H6X2","text":"Plays a role in cell attachment and migration. Interacts with extracellular matrix proteins and with the actin cytoskeleton and thereby plays an important role in normal extracellular matrix (ECM) homeostasis. Mediates adhesion of cells to type 1 collagen and gelatin, reorganization of the actin cytoskeleton and promotes cell spreading. Plays a role in the angiogenic response of cultured umbilical vein endothelial cells. May also act as a receptor for PLAU. Upon ligand binding, stimulates the phosphorylation of EGFR and ERK1/2 (PubMed:30241478). (Microbial infection) Acts as a receptor for protective antigen (PA) of B.anthracis. (Microbial infection) Mediates cell entry of Seneca Valley virus (SVV) when glycosylated."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}